      "name": "ProcessEventQueueWorker",
      "signature": "void ProcessEventQueueWorker(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__d2regcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Event Queue Processing Worker Thread\\n\\nPurpose:\\nContinuously monitors an event queue and processes pending events until shutdown is signaled.\\nThis function runs in a worker thread and waits for events to be queued, then processes them\\none at a time with deadline-based timeout scheduling.\\n\\nAlgorithm:\\n1. Load shutdown flag from EventQueueManager +0x34; if non-zero, set completion flag and return\\n2. Save volatile registers (EBX, EBP, ESI)\\n3. Main event processing loop:\\n   a. Get current system tick count via GetTickCount()\\n   b. Check if queue has pending items (count at +0x2c)\\n   c. If queue empty: wait indefinitely on event handle (+0x3c) for new events\\n   d. If queue has items: calculate wait timeout by subtracting current tick from event deadline (+0x0c)\\n   e. If deadline has passed, timeout is 0; wait with calculated timeout on event handle\\n   f. On successful wait signal: acquire queue lock (CRITICAL_SECTION at +0x14) and process oldest event:\\n      - Remove event from queue using HeapRemoveLastAndSiftDown\\n      - Call event destructor at offset +0x10 for cleanup\\n      - Deallocate event data buffer at offset +0x14\\n      - Deallocate event object memory\\n   g. If WaitForSingleObject returns WAIT_FAILED (-1): call GetLastError() and log error via InitializeNetworkConnection\\n4. Repeat step 3 until shutdown flag (+0x34) becomes non-zero\\n5. Set completion flag (+0x38) = 1 to signal graceful shutdown\\n6. Restore registers and return 0\\n\\nParameters:\\nEDI (IMPLICIT): Pointer to EventQueueManager structure\\n  +0x04: eventCount (uint) - Number of pending events in queue heap\\n  +0x08: pEventArray (Event*) - Pointer to root of binary heap of pending events\\n  +0x0C: eventDeadline (uint) - Tick count deadline for oldest pending event\\n  +0x14: queueLock (CRITICAL_SECTION) - 24-byte synchronization lock protecting queue access\\n  +0x2C: queueState (uint) - Queue state flags/metadata\\n  +0x34: shutdownFlag (uint) - Non-zero signals worker thread to exit\\n  +0x38: completeFlag (uint) - Set to 1 after graceful shutdown to signal completion\\n  +0x3C: eventHandle (HANDLE) - Win32 event object for signaling pending events\\n\\nReturns:\\nAlways returns 0 (status code ignored by caller)\\n\\nSpecial Cases:\\n- If queue is empty, timeout is set to infinite (0xffffffff) to block indefinitely until event arrives\\n- If calculated timeout is negative (deadline has passed), timeout is clamped to 0 for immediate processing\\n- If pEvent is null after queue check due to race condition, cleanup is called on null pointer\\n- WaitForSingleObject errors are caught and logged but do not interrupt the event loop\\n- Shutdown flag is checked at loop start and end to ensure clean exit\\n- CRITICAL_SECTION protection is used to prevent race conditions between queue modifications and timeout calculations",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
