      "name": "PrintCpuVendorAndVersion",
      "signature": "void PrintCpuVendorAndVersion(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Detects CPU type and displays vendor, version, and speed information.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load global CPU type identifier from g_wCpuType\n2. Check if CPU type == 3 (Intel 386):\n   - If true, call ValidateAndInitializeParameter with szIntel386String\n   - Exit function\n3. Check if CPU type == 4 (Intel 486):\n   - If true, call ValidateAndInitializeParameter with szIntel486String\n   - Exit function\n4. For modern CPUs (type > 4):\n   a. Call GetCpuVendorId() to get initial CPU stepping ID\n   b. Call GetCpuVersionInfo() to retrieve complete CPU signature (32-bit value)\n   c. Extract CPU feature fields via bit shifting and masking:\n      - Model: (signature >> 12) & 0x3\n      - Family: (signature >> 8) & 0xf\n      - Type: (signature >> 4) & 0xf\n      - Stepping: lowest 8 bits\n   d. Call GetCpuVendorString() to get vendor string name\n   e. Call GetCpuBrandString() to format CPU info into buffer\n   f. Call ValidateAndInitializeParameter with szVendorFormatString and vendor name\n   g. Call ValidateAndInitializeParameter with szVersionFormatString and all CPU features\n   h. Call MeasureCpuFrequency() to measure CPU speed\n   i. Call ValidateAndInitializeParameter with szSpeedFormatString and frequency value\n\nParameters:\nNone. Function reads CPU information from processor and global CPU type identifier (g_wCpuType).\n\nReturns:\nvoid. Function prints CPU information to output via ValidateAndInitializeParameter calls.\n\nCPU Signature Structure (32-bit, from GetCpuVersionInfo):\nOffset  Size  Field       Type    Description\n0       4     signature   uint32  Complete CPU signature with feature bits\n          Bits 0-7:   Stepping (CPU revision level)\n          Bits 4-7:   Type (processor type ID)\n          Bits 8-11:  Family (processor generation)\n          Bits 12-13: Model (specific variant)\n          Bits 14-31: Reserved/Extended features\n\nVendor String Array (g_pszCpuVendorStrings):\nA 4-byte-aligned array of pointers to vendor string names, indexed by cpuSteppingValue.\nArray entries map processor IDs to vendor/brand strings like \"Pentium Pro\", \"Pentium II\", etc.\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Magic values 3 and 4 represent legacy Intel 386/486 CPUs with fixed output strings\n- Vendor index derived from cpuSteppingValue is used to look up vendor string in g_pszCpuVendorStrings\n- CPU signature bit fields allow extraction of processor generation, model, and type info\n- Stack allocation of 0x108 bytes (264 bytes) accommodates cpuInfoBuffer (256 bytes) + temp variables",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
