      "name": "ApplyCharacterTransformationToBuffer",
      "signature": "void ApplyCharacterTransformationToBuffer(char * pBuffer, int bufferLength)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Apply character transformation to buffer using lookup table.\n\nTransforms each character in a buffer by looking up its value in a 256-byte lookup table (g_CharLookupTable at 0x6ff76690). The lookup table performs byte-to-byte character mapping, typically for case conversion or character encoding transformation. Processing stops when encountering a null terminator or reaching the buffer length boundary, whichever comes first.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate end pointer as pBuffer + bufferLength\n2. Load first character from buffer at [pBuffer]\n3. Loop while character != 0x00 AND pBuffer < end pointer:\n   a. Load character byte value from [pBuffer]\n   b. Lookup replacement value in table: g_CharLookupTable[charValue]\n   c. Write transformed byte back to [pBuffer]\n   d. Advance pBuffer to next character (INC ECX)\n   e. Load next character from [pBuffer+1] for condition test\n4. Exit loop and return when null terminator found or length boundary reached\n\nParameters:\npBuffer (ECX): Pointer to buffer containing characters to transform\nbufferLength (EDX): Maximum number of bytes to process\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Transformation applied in-place to buffer\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Processing halts at first 0x00 null terminator even if bufferLength not consumed\n- Processing halts at length boundary even if no null terminator exists\n- Table at g_CharLookupTable (0x6ff76690) is 256 bytes supporting full byte range (0x00-0xFF)\n- This lookup table typically maps uppercase letters (0x41-0x5A) to lowercase (0x61-0x7A)\n- All transformations are 1:1 byte mappings with no state maintained\n\nStructure Layout:\nThe g_CharLookupTable is a simple 256-byte character translation table where:\nOffset 0x00-0x3F: ASCII control chars and symbols (typically identity or encoding-specific)\nOffset 0x40 (0x41-0x5A): Uppercase letters mapped to lowercase (0x61-0x7A)\nOffset 0x5B-0x7F: Special chars and numbers (typically identity)\nOffset 0x80-0xFF: Extended ASCII (preserved as-is for encoding compatibility)",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
      "method": "MNE",
