      "name": "CreateDirectoryPathRecursive",
      "signature": "uint CreateDirectoryPathRecursive(char * pPath)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "uint",
      "comment": "Recursively creates all intermediate directories in a file path.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Validate input path (non-null, non-empty)\n2. Search for first backslash character starting from position 1\n3. Loop through each backslash-delimited path component:\n   a. Copy path substring from start to current backslash position into local buffer (268 bytes)\n   b. Null-terminate the substring at the backslash position\n   c. Attempt CreateDirectoryA() with NULL security attributes\n   d. If directory creation succeeds, continue to next component\n   e. If directory creation fails, check GetLastError():\n      - If error is 0xB7 (ERROR_ALREADY_EXISTS) or 0x5 (ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED)\n      - Call GetFileAttributesA() to verify path is actually a directory (attribute 0x10)\n      - If directory exists, continue to next component\n      - Otherwise, fail and return 0\n   f. Search for next backslash after current position\n   g. Continue until all components processed or path exceeds 260 bytes (0x104)\n4. Return 1 if successful, 0 if any creation or validation failed\n\nParameters:\n  pPath (ECX register) [char*]: Pointer to null-terminated absolute file path\n                                Format: \"C:\\\\DirectoryA\\\\DirectoryB\\\\filename.ext\"\n                                Path must contain at least one backslash after first character\n                                Maximum path length: 259 bytes (MAX_PATH - 1)\n\nReturns:\n  1 (TRUE)  - All directory levels successfully created or already exist\n  0 (FALSE) - Path validation failed, path too long, or directory creation failed\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - NULL or empty string input returns 0 immediately\n  - Path length must be less than 260 bytes (0x104) to be processed\n  - CreateDirectoryA error codes 0xB7 and 0x5 are acceptable if directory verifiably exists\n  - Uses FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY (0x10) check to distinguish directories from files\n  - Uses stack-allocated 268-byte buffer (0x10C) for path manipulation\n  - Uses __fastcall convention: first parameter in ECX register, caller cleanup",
