      "name": "CleanupCriticalSection",
      "signature": "void CleanupCriticalSection(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Cleans up and destroys a critical section synchronization object.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize the critical section with Ordinal_10251 passing address of global critical section data at 0x6ff816d8\n2. Enter the critical section via EnterCriticalSection to acquire the lock\n3. Delete/destroy the critical section via DeleteCriticalSection to release resources\n4. Return to caller\n\nParameters:\nNone - Operates on a fixed global critical section object at 0x6ff816d8\n\nReturns:\nvoid\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- The function performs cleanup operations in sequence: init, enter, then delete\n- Uses a global CRITICAL_SECTION structure rather than passing it as a parameter\n- EnterCriticalSection is called immediately before DeleteCriticalSection, which is unusual cleanup pattern\n- The critical section is located at offset 0x6ff816d8 in the global data segment\n\nStructure Layout:\nThe CRITICAL_SECTION structure at 0x6ff816d8 appears to be Windows CRITICAL_SECTION (24+ bytes)\nAccess Pattern shows size approximately 24 bytes based on xref analysis",
