      "signature": "void ProcessNetworkPackets(void * pCurrentPacketPos, int nBufferSize)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Processes incoming network packets from shared buffer and invokes handler callback.\n\nThis function implements a packet processing loop that reads variable-length packets from a\nshared memory buffer, validates packet sizes, and dispatches them to a registered callback\nhandler or default enqueue handler. Manages receive event signaling for thread coordination.\n\nALGORITHM:\n1. Initialize packet position pointer to buffer start address\n2. Main packet processing loop:\n   a. Read 16-bit packet size from buffer at current position\n   b. Calculate remaining buffer space (bufferEnd - currentPos + bufferSize)\n   c. Validate packet size fits within remaining space\n   d. Check maximum packet size limit (0x2063 bytes) - exit with error 0xFB if exceeded\n   e. Fetch callback handler pointer from context structure (EBX+0x168)\n   f. If no callback (null pointer), use default EnqueuePacketToQueue handler\n   g. If callback exists, validate it's executable with IsBadCodePtr\n   h. If invalid callback, exit with error 0x101 and shutdown\n   i. Check count threshold at EBX+0x11C (< 1 vs >= 1) to determine parameter source\n   j. Invoke callback handler with appropriate packet data pointer\n   k. On success (return 0), queue packet and signal receive event\n   l. Advance packet position by packet size and loop to next packet\n3. On buffer underrun (insufficient data for next packet), copy remaining data and return\n\nPARAMETERS:\n  pCurrentPacketPos (void*) - Current read position in packet buffer\n  nBufferSize (int) - Total size of buffer or end position marker\n\nRETURNS:\n  void - Function returns after processing all complete packets or on buffer underrun\n\nSPECIAL CASES:\n  - Packet size 0x2063+ bytes triggers FATAL_PACKET_SIZE_EXCEEDED error (0xFB)\n  - Custom callback validation uses IsBadCodePtr API\n  - Invalid callback triggers CALLBACK_POINTER_INVALID error (0x101)\n  - Count threshold (EBX+0x11C) determines parameter pass to callback\n  - Buffer underrun copies remaining unaligned data in DWORD chunks then remainder bytes\n  - SetEvent called on EBX+0x114 handle to signal packet reception thread\n\nIMPLICIT CONTEXT REGISTERS (via EBX):\n  EBX+0x114 - HANDLE to receive event (signaled after each packet processed)\n  EBX+0x118 - Base offset for circular buffer address calculation\n  EBX+0x11C - Count threshold (< 1: low count path, >= 1: high count path)\n  EBX+0x168 - Function pointer to custom callback handler (NULL if using default)\n  EBX+0x16C - Callback context parameter\n  EBX+0x170 - Buffer for default handler EnqueuePacketToQueue\n\nNOTE: Function receives context via EBX register (implicit parameter) rather than stack,\nenabling access to network state and callback configuration without parameter overhead.",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.12a",
      "method": "CAL",
      "index": "CAL:3163c63dfa4aa4036ddf2ab733c410d7",
