      "name": "GetProtectedObjectValue",
      "signature": "uint GetProtectedObjectValue(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "uint",
      "comment": "Atomically reads a protected value from a threadsafe object.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Save ESI and EDI registers on stack for safe use\n2. Copy pObject parameter into EDI\n3. Calculate critical section address at pObject+4 into ESI\n4. Call ValidateDataStructureIntegrity(0x3a) to validate structure\n5. Enter critical section by calling EnterCriticalSection(pCriticalSection)\n6. Read the 32-bit value from pObject base address into EDI\n7. Leave critical section by calling LeaveCriticalSection(pCriticalSection)\n8. Move result to EAX and restore registers\n9. Return to caller (stack cleanup: 4 bytes parameter)\n\nParameters:\npObject - Pointer to a thread-protected object structure containing:\n  - [offset 0x0] uint: Protected value (4 bytes)\n  - [offset 0x4] CRITICAL_SECTION: Windows critical section lock (24 bytes)\n\nReturns:\nuint - The 32-bit value read from the protected object\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Magic number 0x3a (58) passed to ValidateDataStructureIntegrity for validation\n- Uses Windows CRITICAL_SECTION for thread-safe access\n- __stdcall convention: callee cleanup, function RET 0x4\n- Value may change between read and return due to concurrent access\n- Callers should not rely on value stability across function calls\n\nStructure Layout:\nOffset | Size | Field Name | Type | Description\n0x00   | 4    | dwValue    | uint | Protected 32-bit value\n0x04   | 24   | csLock     | CRITICAL_SECTION | Windows critical section structure",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
      "method": "CAL",
      "index": "CAL:62ea6eefb994ddcf321760239c499701",
